(window.SERVICE_BANKS = window.SERVICE_BANKS || {})["dab"] = {"name":"Data API builder","icon":"🔌","blurb":"Data API builder 2.0 from A to Z: the configuration file, the dab CLI, entities, authentication and authorization, REST and GraphQL endpoints, SQL MCP Server, caching, health, telemetry and deployment.","order":2,"sourceNote":"Written from the Microsoft Learn documentation; every explanation links the page it was checked against.","sections":[{"id":"1","name":"What Data API builder is"},{"id":"2","name":"The configuration file"},{"id":"3","name":"The dab CLI"},{"id":"4","name":"Entities"},{"id":"5","name":"Authentication"},{"id":"6","name":"Authorization"},{"id":"7","name":"REST endpoints"},{"id":"8","name":"GraphQL endpoints"},{"id":"9","name":"SQL MCP Server"},{"id":"10","name":"Runtime: host, caching, health and telemetry"},{"id":"11","name":"Deployment"}],"questions":[{"id":"dab-1.1-1","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 1.1 · Overview and architecture","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about Data API builder (DAB).","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"DAB is an open-source, configuration-based engine that replaces generic CRUD APIs. It serves REST and GraphQL simultaneously (and MCP since 1.7), runs **statelessly** anywhere (on-premises or any cloud) and is Docker-friendly. Built-in features include pagination, filtering, sorting, projection, stored procedures, relationships and aggregation for SQL databases.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Open source and free:** the engine, CLI and container image cost nothing. You only pay for wherever you host it and for the database.\n- **No application code:** with an entity `Book` on `dbo.Books` in `dab-config.json`, `dab start` serves `GET /api/Book` and `POST /graphql` with `{ books { items { title } } }`. There are no controllers to write.\n- **REST and GraphQL together:** both are on by default and serve the same entities with the same permissions. You turn either off with `runtime.rest.enabled` or `runtime.graphql.enabled`.\n- **Stateful, can't run as a container** is false. DAB keeps no session state, so\n\n  ```bash\n  docker run -p 5000:5000 -v ./dab-config.json:/App/dab-config.json \\\n    mcr.microsoft.com/azure-databases/data-api-builder\n  ```\n\n  works, and you can run three replicas behind a load balancer.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["DAB is open source and free, with no premium tier.","DAB generates REST and GraphQL endpoints from a configuration file, without application code.","REST and GraphQL can be enabled at the same time.","DAB is stateful, so it can't run as a container."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"dab-1.1-2","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 1.1 · Overview and architecture","scenario":"","stem":"In DAB's architecture, what does the configuration file add between the database schema and the API?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The config projects tables, views and stored procedures into **entities**. That layer lets you rename objects, alias or hide columns, add relationships (even ones the database doesn't declare) and set role permissions, so the API doesn't leak the raw schema. At runtime DAB reads the config and builds the queries.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Entities:** for example, `dbo.tbl_bk_master` becomes the entity `Book`, the column `bk_ttl` is exposed as `title`, `cost_price` is excluded, and only the `editor` role may update. Clients never see the table's real names.\n- **A copy of the data** is false. DAB queries the database on each request (or serves a short-lived cache, if you turn caching on). It has no data store of its own.\n- **Generated C# controllers** is false. Nothing is generated or compiled. DAB reads the config at startup and builds the endpoints in memory.\n- **Stored procedures created in the database** is false. DAB never changes your schema. It sends its own parameterized T-SQL, such as `SELECT [title] FROM [dbo].[tbl_bk_master] WHERE [id] = @param0`.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"An abstraction layer of entities: you name them, alias or limit fields, define relationships and apply permissions"},{"key":"B","text":"A copy of the data that DAB serves instead of the database"},{"key":"C","text":"Generated C# controllers that you compile"},{"key":"D","text":"Stored procedures that DAB creates in the database"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-1.1-3","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 1.1 · Overview and architecture","scenario":"","stem":"Which developer tools does DAB provide for testing its endpoints?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"DAB includes a cross-platform CLI, OpenTelemetry and health checks, plus **OpenAPI/Swagger** for REST and **Nitro** for GraphQL; both UIs are enabled in development mode. A VS Code extension adds init, add, start, validate, health, visualize and code generation commands.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Swagger UI and Nitro:** in development mode, open `http://localhost:5000/swagger` to try REST calls, or browse to `http://localhost:5000/graphql` to get Nitro, an IDE with schema docs and autocomplete.\n- **Postman collections generated in the database** don't exist. DAB stores nothing in your database. You can import the OpenAPI document from `/api/openapi` into Postman yourself.\n- **SSMS plug-ins** don't exist. SSMS works with the database, not with DAB's API.\n- **GraphiQL hosted by Azure** isn't part of DAB. The GraphQL IDE runs inside DAB itself (Nitro), wherever you host it.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Swagger UI for REST and Nitro (formerly Banana Cake Pop) for GraphQL"},{"key":"B","text":"Postman collections generated in the database"},{"key":"C","text":"SQL Server Management Studio plug-ins"},{"key":"D","text":"GraphiQL hosted by Azure"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-1.2-1","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 1.2 · Supported databases and what's new in 2.0","scenario":"","stem":"Match each data source to its `database-type` value.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The values are `mssql`, `dwsql`, `postgresql` (version 11+), `mysql` (version 8+), `cosmosdb_nosql` and `cosmosdb_postgresql`. Cosmos DB for NoSQL also needs `options.database`, `options.container` and a GraphQL `schema` file. Some features (such as aggregation and autoentities) are SQL-only.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`mssql`** covers SQL Server 2016+, Azure SQL Database, Azure SQL Managed Instance and SQL database in Fabric.\n- **`dwsql`:** Fabric Warehouse and Azure Synapse Analytics, whose T-SQL differs enough to need their own query builder.\n- **`cosmosdb_nosql`:** GraphQL only, with a schema file (it has its own question).\n- **`postgresql`:** PostgreSQL 11 or later. `mysql` (8+) and `cosmosdb_postgresql` are the other values.\n- **`fabric`** and **`sqlserver`** aren't values. A SQL database in Fabric is `mssql`, and a Fabric Warehouse is `dwsql`. With an unknown value, `dab validate` fails at the schema stage.\n\n**Source:** [data source](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/data-source) · [version 2 0](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/whats-new/version-2-0)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Azure SQL Database, SQL Server 2016+ or SQL database in Fabric","Fabric Warehouse or Azure Synapse Analytics","Azure Cosmos DB for NoSQL","PostgreSQL 11+"],"values":["cosmosdb_nosql","dwsql","fabric","mssql","postgresql","sqlserver"],"answer":["mssql","dwsql","cosmosdb_nosql","postgresql"]}},{"id":"dab-1.2-2","domain":2,"section":"1","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 1.2 · Supported databases and what's new in 2.0","scenario":"","stem":"Which features are new in DAB 2.0? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"DAB 2.0 focuses on MCP and AI, simpler authentication defaults and configuration automation: **autoentities**, **role inheritance**, **OBO** to Microsoft SQL, **permission-filtered OpenAPI**, the `aggregate_records` MCP tool and more. GraphQL has been supported since the first GA release.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Autoentities and `dab auto-config`**, **role inheritance** and **OBO** each have their own questions.\n- **Role-aware OpenAPI:** if `anonymous` can only read `Book`, the OpenAPI document an anonymous client gets lists only `GET /api/Book`, without the POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE operations or excluded fields.\n- **The first support for GraphQL** is false. GraphQL has been there since the first release, alongside REST.\n\n**Source:** [data source](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/data-source) · [version 2 0](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/whats-new/version-2-0)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Autoentities and `dab auto-config`"},{"key":"B","text":"Role inheritance"},{"key":"C","text":"On-Behalf-Of (OBO) user-delegated authentication to SQL"},{"key":"D","text":"Role-aware OpenAPI documents"},{"key":"E","text":"The first support for GraphQL"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-2.1-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 2.1 · File structure and $schema","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about the DAB configuration file.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Top-level properties: `$schema`, `data-source` (required), `data-source-files`, `runtime`, `entities`, `autoentities` and `azure-key-vault`. When `autoentities` is present, `entities` isn't required; if both define the same name, the explicit entity wins. The latest schema is at `…/releases/latest/download/dab.draft.schema.json`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\nA minimal file:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://github.com/Azure/data-api-builder/releases/latest/download/dab.draft.schema.json\",\n  \"data-source\": { \"database-type\": \"mssql\", \"connection-string\": \"@env('SQL_CONN')\" },\n  \"autoentities\": { \"...\": \"...\" }\n}\n```\n\n- **`$schema`:** VS Code then autocompletes property names and underlines typos such as `\"premissions\"`.\n- **`data-source` is required:** without it, DAB has nothing to connect to, and validation fails.\n- **`entities` is required even with `autoentities`** is false. The file above, with no `entities` at all, is valid. If you do add an `entities` section, an entity there with the same name as an autoentity overrides it.\n- **`azure-key-vault`** sits at the top level, next to `data-source`, with the vault's `endpoint`, so `@akv()` can resolve secrets.\n\n**Source:** [configuration](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["It starts with a `$schema` property that enables schema validation.","`data-source` is required.","You must list `entities`, even if you use `autoentities`.","`azure-key-vault` is a top-level property."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"dab-2.2-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 2.2 · The data-source section","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the data source so DAB connects to Azure SQL with its **system-assigned managed identity**, reads the server name from an environment variable, and sends JWT claims to the database session.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"For a **system-assigned** identity, add only `Authentication=Active Directory Managed Identity;` (no user ID or password); for a **user-assigned** one, also add `User Id=<client-id>`. DAB uses `DefaultAzureCredential` from Azure.Identity. `set-session-context` (mssql only) passes claims to `SESSION_CONTEXT`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`azuresql`** and **`sqlserver`** aren't database types. Azure SQL and SQL Server are both `mssql`.\n- **`...;User Id=<client-id>;`** is for a **user-assigned** identity, where you have to say which one. A system-assigned identity needs no ID, because the app has only one.\n- **`Integrated Security=true;`** is Windows authentication, for a domain-joined machine talking to an on-premises SQL Server. It doesn't work with Azure SQL.\n- **`send-claims`** and **`session-context`** aren't options. With `set-session-context: true`, DAB runs `EXEC sp_set_session_context 'roles', 'editor'` (and the token's other claims) before each query, so T-SQL can read `SESSION_CONTEXT(N'roles')`.\n\n**Source:** [data source](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/data-source)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"{\n  \"data-source\": {\n    \"database-type\": \"[[0]]\",\n    \"connection-string\": \"Server=tcp:@env('SQL_SERVER'),1433;Database=Library;[[1]]\",\n    \"options\": { \"[[2]]\": true }\n  }\n}","options":[["azuresql","mssql","sqlserver"],["Authentication=Active Directory Managed Identity;","Authentication=Active Directory Managed Identity;User Id=<client-id>;","Integrated Security=true;"],["send-claims","session-context","set-session-context"]],"answer":["mssql","Authentication=Active Directory Managed Identity;","set-session-context"]}},{"id":"dab-2.2-2","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 2.2 · The data-source section","scenario":"","stem":"Your data source is Azure Cosmos DB for NoSQL. Which options must you set besides the connection string?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Cosmos DB for NoSQL needs `options.database`, usually `options.container`, and `options.schema` pointing at a GraphQL schema file, because the documents have no fixed schema. REST settings are ignored for `cosmosdb_nosql` (it's GraphQL-only). `dab export --graphql --generate` can sample data to produce that schema.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`database`, `container`, `schema`:**\n\n  ```json\n  \"data-source\": {\n    \"database-type\": \"cosmosdb_nosql\",\n    \"connection-string\": \"@env('COSMOS_CONN')\",\n    \"options\": { \"database\": \"Library\", \"container\": \"books\", \"schema\": \"schema.gql\" }\n  }\n  ```\n\n  and `schema.gql` declares the shape, for example `type Book @model { id: ID!, title: String, year: Int }`.\n- **Only `set-session-context`:** that option is for `mssql` only. Cosmos DB has no session context.\n- **`schema` and `key-fields`:** `key-fields` is a SQL entity setting for views. Cosmos DB uses the document `id` and the container's partition key.\n- **Nothing, inferred from the container** is false. Documents have no fixed schema, so DAB needs the GraphQL schema file. `dab export --graphql --generate` can sample documents to draft one.\n\n**Source:** [data source](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/data-source)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`database`, `container` (depending on entities) and the path to a GraphQL `schema` file"},{"key":"B","text":"Only `set-session-context`"},{"key":"C","text":"`schema` and `key-fields`"},{"key":"D","text":"Nothing else; DAB infers everything from the container"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-2.3-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 2.3 · Secrets: @env() and @akv()","scenario":"","stem":"You don't want the connection string in plain text in `dab-config.json`. Which value loads it from an environment variable?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The **`@env('NAME')`** function substitutes an environment variable when the config loads. DAB also reads a `.env` file (a file with no name, only the extension) in the working folder, which is handy locally. Never commit secrets to the config.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`@env('SQL_CONNECTION_STRING')`:** locally, put `SQL_CONNECTION_STRING=Server=localhost;Database=Library;Trusted_Connection=True;TrustServerCertificate=True` in a `.env` file next to the config (and in `.gitignore`). In Azure, set the same variable on the container or app.\n- **`$SQL_CONNECTION_STRING`** is shell syntax. DAB doesn't expand it, so it tries to connect with the literal string `$SQL_CONNECTION_STRING` and fails.\n- **`{{SQL_CONNECTION_STRING}}`** is template syntax from other tools (Helm, Mustache). DAB has no such templating.\n- **`env:SQL_CONNECTION_STRING`** isn't a DAB syntax either. `@env('...')` and `@akv('...')` are the only two functions.\n\n**Source:** [env function](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/config/env-function) · [akv function](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/config/akv-function)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`\"connection-string\": \"@env('SQL_CONNECTION_STRING')\"`"},{"key":"B","text":"`\"connection-string\": \"$SQL_CONNECTION_STRING\"`"},{"key":"C","text":"`\"connection-string\": \"{{SQL_CONNECTION_STRING}}\"`"},{"key":"D","text":"`\"connection-string\": \"env:SQL_CONNECTION_STRING\"`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-2.3-2","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 2.3 · Secrets: @env() and @akv()","scenario":"","stem":"You use `\"connection-string\": \"@akv('sql-conn')\"`. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`@akv('name')` resolves a Key Vault secret **at configuration load time**, like `@env()`. Configure `azure-key-vault.endpoint` and optionally a `retry-policy` (mode, max-count, delay-seconds, max-delay-seconds). Missing secrets or authorization errors stop the config from loading.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`azure-key-vault` section:**\n\n  ```json\n  \"azure-key-vault\": {\n    \"endpoint\": \"https://kv-library.vault.azure.net/\",\n    \"retry-policy\": { \"mode\": \"exponential\", \"max-count\": 3 }\n  }\n  ```\n\n  DAB reads the secret with its own identity, such as a managed identity with **Key Vault Secrets User**.\n- **Loading fails when the secret can't be read:** for example, if the identity lacks access or the secret's name is wrong, `dab start` stops at startup rather than running without a database.\n- **A local `.akv` file** holds stand-in secret values for development, so you don't need a real vault or network access on your laptop.\n- **Fetched on every request** is false. The secret is read once, when the configuration loads. After rotating `sql-conn`, restart DAB (or reload the config) to use the new value.\n\n**Source:** [env function](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/config/env-function) · [akv function](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/config/akv-function)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["You must add an `azure-key-vault` section with the vault's `endpoint`.","If the secret can't be retrieved, configuration loading fails.","A local `.akv` file can satisfy `@akv()` lookups during development without calling Key Vault.","`@akv()` secrets are fetched again on every API request."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"dab-2.4-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 2.4 · Environments and multiple configuration files","scenario":"","stem":"You have `dab-config.json`, `dab-config.Development.json` and `dab-config.Production.json`. Which statements are true? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Environment files work like ASP.NET Core `appsettings.json`: the base file is shared, and the `dab-config.<env>.json` file **overrides** it when `DAB_ENVIRONMENT` is set (typically just a different `@env()` connection string). `dab validate` resolves a single file.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`DAB_ENVIRONMENT=Development`:** `dab-config.Development.json` can contain only `{ \"data-source\": { \"connection-string\": \"@env('DEV_SQL')\" } }`, and its values override the base file's. Everything else comes from `dab-config.json`.\n- **Which file `dab start` uses:** with `DAB_ENVIRONMENT=Production` and a `dab-config.Production.json`, that one is used. With no matching file, `dab-config.json` alone.\n- **`dab validate` checks one resolved file:** run it with the same `DAB_ENVIRONMENT` as the target, so CI validates what production will actually load.\n- **Repeat every entity** is false. The environment file holds only the differences, usually just the connection string.\n\n**Source:** [environments](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/config/environments) · [configuration](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Setting `DAB_ENVIRONMENT=Development` makes DAB use the Development file's values over the base file"},{"key":"B","text":"`dab start` uses `dab-config.<DAB_ENVIRONMENT>.json` when it exists, otherwise `dab-config.json`"},{"key":"C","text":"`dab validate` checks one resolved file and doesn't merge environment variants"},{"key":"D","text":"The environment file must repeat every entity from the base file"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-2.4-2","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 2.4 · Environments and multiple configuration files","scenario":"","stem":"A team splits its entities across files with `data-source-files`. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Multiple files let DAB connect to several data sources at once. Every file needs `data-source` and `entities` (or `autoentities`); `runtime` must be in the top-level file and is ignored in child files. Entity names must be unique across all files, and **cross-file relationships aren't supported**.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\nFor example, `dab-config.json` contains `\"data-source-files\": [\"sales.json\", \"inventory.json\"]`.\n\n- **Each file has a `data-source`:** `sales.json` can point at the Sales database and `inventory.json` at a PostgreSQL inventory database. That's how one DAB instance serves several databases.\n- **Only the top-level `runtime`:** a `runtime` section in `sales.json` is ignored. CORS, authentication and paths come from `dab-config.json`.\n- **`Book` in two files** is false. Entity names must be unique across all files, because they become one set of REST routes and GraphQL types.\n- **Cross-file relationships** is false. `Order` in `sales.json` can't have a relationship to `Product` in `inventory.json`. The client has to call both and join the results itself.\n\n**Source:** [environments](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/config/environments) · [configuration](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Each configuration file must include a `data-source`.","Only the top-level file's `runtime` settings are used.","Two files can each define an entity named `Book`.","You can define a relationship between entities that live in different files."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","No"]}},{"id":"dab-2.5-1","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 2.5 · Autoentities and auto-config","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the command that exposes every `dbo` table except the internal ones, named `{schema}_{object}`, readable by anonymous users.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`autoentities` (DAB 2.0, **MSSQL tables only**) exposes objects matching `include`/`exclude` patterns (SQL `LIKE` style with `%`), names them with a pattern such as `{schema}_{object}`, and applies a template (REST, GraphQL, MCP, health, cache) plus permissions. `dab auto-config-simulate` connects to the database and lists the matches, optionally to CSV with `--output`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`filter`** isn't a pattern key. Patterns are `include`, `exclude` and `name`.\n- **`read:anonymous`** has the parts in the wrong order. Permissions are always written `role:actions`, as in `anonymous:read` or `editor:create,read,update`.\n- **`{object}`** would name the entities `Books` and `Authors`. That works here, but if `sales.Orders` and `dbo.Orders` were both included, they'd collide. `{schema}_{object}` gives `dbo_Books`, which is the naming this question asks for.\n- **`validate`** checks a configuration file. **`auto-config-simulate`** connects to the database and lists which tables the patterns match, such as `dbo.Books → dbo_Books`, without changing anything. Add `--output matches.csv` for a file.\n\n**Source:** [autoentities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/autoentities) · [version 2 0](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/whats-new/version-2-0)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"dab auto-config my-def \\\n  --patterns.[[0]] \"dbo.%\" \\\n  --patterns.[[1]] \"dbo.internal%\" \\\n  --patterns.name \"[[2]]\" \\\n  --permissions \"[[3]]\"\n\ndab [[4]]   # preview which objects match","values":["anonymous:read","auto-config-simulate","exclude","filter","include","read:anonymous","validate","{object}","{schema}_{object}"],"answer":["include","exclude","{schema}_{object}","anonymous:read","auto-config-simulate"]}},{"id":"dab-2.5-2","domain":2,"section":"2","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 2.5 · Autoentities and auto-config","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about `autoentities`.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Autoentities re-evaluate patterns at startup to expose matching objects with a template and permissions. Microsoft stresses it isn't a schema-driven API: you still choose what's exposed through include/exclude patterns, naming and permissions, so the API doesn't simply mirror the schema.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **MSSQL only:** on a `postgresql` data source, an `autoentities` section doesn't expose anything.\n- **Tables only in 2.0:** a pattern `dbo.%` matches `dbo.Books` but not the view `dbo.vBookSummary` or a stored procedure. Define those as explicit entities.\n- **Explicit entities win:** an `autoentities` match named `dbo_Books` and an explicit `dbo_Books` entity with stricter permissions resolve to the explicit one.\n- **Exposes every object automatically** is false. Only objects that match the include patterns, minus the excludes, are exposed, with the permissions you set. A new table `dbo.internal_audit` stays hidden if `dbo.internal%` is excluded.\n\n**Source:** [autoentities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/autoentities) · [version 2 0](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/whats-new/version-2-0)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Autoentities support MSSQL data sources only.","In 2.0, `dab auto-config` covers tables only.","An explicitly defined entity takes precedence over an autoentities match with the same name.","Autoentities turn DAB into a schema-driven API that exposes every object automatically."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"dab-3.1-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 3.1 · Installing the CLI and dab init","scenario":"","stem":"How is the DAB CLI installed?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The CLI ships as the **`Microsoft.DataApiBuilder`** .NET tool (it needs .NET 8). Update it with `dotnet tool update`, check it with `dab --version`, and on Linux or macOS add the .NET global tools folder to `PATH` if `dab` isn't found.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **.NET tool:** `dotnet tool install --global Microsoft.DataApiBuilder`, then `dab --version`. If the shell says `dab: command not found` on Linux, add `~/.dotnet/tools` to `PATH`.\n- **npm** and **pip** don't have DAB packages.\n- **Only a Docker image** is false. There *is* an official engine image (`mcr.microsoft.com/azure-databases/data-api-builder`) for hosting, but the CLI that creates and edits configs is the .NET tool.\n\n**Source:** [install](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/install) · [dab init](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-init)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"As a .NET tool: `dotnet tool install --global Microsoft.DataApiBuilder`"},{"key":"B","text":"With `npm install -g data-api-builder`"},{"key":"C","text":"With `pip install dab`"},{"key":"D","text":"Only as a Docker image"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-3.1-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 3.1 · Installing the CLI and dab init","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the command that creates a configuration for SQL Server, reading the connection string from an environment variable, in development mode with Entra ID authentication.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`dab init` creates `dab-config.json` (or the file in `-c`). Options include `--database-type`, `--connection-string`, `--host-mode` (default Production), `--auth.provider` (default `Unauthenticated`) with `--auth.audience` and `--auth.issuer`, `--rest.path`, `--graphql.path`, `--mcp.enabled` and `--set-session-context`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`add`** creates an *entity* in a config that already exists. **`configure`** changes runtime or data-source settings of an existing config. Only **`init`** creates the file.\n- **`azure-sql`** and **`sqlserver`** aren't database types. Both SQL Server and Azure SQL are `mssql`.\n- **`Debug`** and **`Local`** aren't host modes. The only modes are `production` (default) and `development`, which turns on Swagger, Nitro and detailed errors.\n- **`AzureAD-v2`** and **`Microsoft`** aren't providers. Entra ID is `EntraId` (older configs say `AzureAD`, which still works).\n\n**Source:** [install](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/install) · [dab init](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-init)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"dab [[0]] \\\n  --database-type [[1]] \\\n  --connection-string \"@env('SQL_CONNECTION_STRING')\" \\\n  --host-mode [[2]] \\\n  --auth.provider [[3]]","options":[["add","configure","init"],["azure-sql","mssql","sqlserver"],["Debug","Development","Local"],["AzureAD-v2","EntraId","Microsoft"]],"answer":["init","mssql","Development","EntraId"]}},{"id":"dab-3.1-3","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 3.1 · Installing the CLI and dab init","scenario":"","stem":"You run `dab init ... --runtime.base-route /v1`. What else does this option require?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"`--runtime.base-route` sets a global prefix for all endpoints and requires `--auth.provider StaticWebApps`, the provider used when DAB runs as the database connection of Azure Static Web Apps.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`StaticWebApps`:** when DAB runs as an Azure Static Web Apps *database connection*, SWA serves it under a prefix (such as `/data-api`) and handles sign-in. The base route exists for that setup, so it requires the `StaticWebApps` provider.\n- **Development host mode** isn't required. The base route works in either mode.\n- **MCP disabled** isn't required. The base route is about the URL prefix, not MCP.\n- **Nothing** is false. With any other provider, `dab validate` rejects the base route.\n\n**Source:** [install](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/install) · [dab init](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-init)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The authentication provider must be `StaticWebApps`"},{"key":"B","text":"Host mode must be development"},{"key":"C","text":"MCP must be disabled"},{"key":"D","text":"Nothing"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-3.2-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 3.2 · dab add and dab update","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the commands: first expose the `dbo.BookSummary` view (key column `id`) read-only to anonymous users, then later add the `title` alias for column `book_title` on the existing entity.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`dab add` creates a new entity (the config must exist from `dab init`); `--permissions` is required as `role:actions`. Views have no intrinsic primary key, so give key fields (`--source.key-fields`, or `fields` with `primary-key` in 2.0). Use **`dab update`** to change an existing entity, and `dab configure` for runtime or data-source settings.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`init`** creates the config file, and **`configure`** changes runtime or data-source settings. Neither adds or changes an entity.\n- **`table`** is wrong for a view. With `--source.type view`, DAB knows it needs key fields, because views have no primary key.\n- **`anonymous:*`** on a view would allow create, update and delete too (DAB writes through updatable views), but the requirement is *read-only*.\n\n`update` is the way to change an entity that already exists: running `dab add BookSummary` a second time fails because the entity is already there.\n\n**Source:** [dab add](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-add) · [dab update](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-update)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"dab [[0]] BookSummary \\\n  --source dbo.BookSummary \\\n  --source.type [[1]] \\\n  --source.key-fields \"[[2]]\" \\\n  --permissions \"[[3]]\"\n\ndab [[4]] BookSummary --fields.name book_title --fields.alias title","values":["add","anonymous:*","anonymous:read","configure","id","init","table","update","view"],"answer":["add","view","id","anonymous:read","update"]}},{"id":"dab-3.2-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 3.2 · dab add and dab update","scenario":"","stem":"You run `dab add Book --source dbo.Books` with no other options. What happens?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"`dab add` requires `--source` and **`--permissions`** (`role:actions`, for one role per command; add more roles with `dab update`). Other options cover REST/GraphQL exposure, caching, fields, policies (`--policy-database`, `--policy-request`), MCP and stored-procedure parameters.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Fails: `--permissions` is required.** DAB won't create an entity that nobody can use. `dab add Book --source dbo.Books --permissions \"anonymous:read\"` works.\n- **Anonymous read by default** is false. DAB never grants access you didn't ask for.\n- **No permissions** is false. DAB refuses to create such an entity, because it would be useless.\n- **Asks interactively** is false. The CLI is meant for scripts and CI, so it just fails with an error message.\n\nAdd more roles afterwards with `dab update Book --permissions \"editor:create,read,update\"`.\n\n**Source:** [dab add](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-add) · [dab update](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-update)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It fails, because `--permissions` is required"},{"key":"B","text":"Book is added with anonymous read access"},{"key":"C","text":"Book is added with no permissions"},{"key":"D","text":"DAB asks interactively for permissions"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-3.3-1","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 3.3 · dab configure, validate, start and export","scenario":"","stem":"Match each command to its purpose.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`dab configure` edits non-entity settings and fails entirely if any option is invalid. `dab validate` is made for CI/CD. `dab start` accepts `--LogLevel`, `--verbose`, `--no-https-redirect` and `--mcp-stdio`. `dab export --graphql` can also **generate** a schema for Cosmos DB for NoSQL by sampling data.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`dab configure`:** `dab configure --runtime.rest.path /rest --data-source.connection-string \"@env('SQL_CONN')\"`. If either value were invalid, neither would be applied.\n- **`dab validate`:** in a pipeline, `dab validate -c dab-config.json` exits non-zero on the first failing stage, which fails the job before a broken config is deployed.\n- **`dab start`:** `dab start --LogLevel Debug` runs the engine locally with verbose logs. `--mcp-stdio` runs MCP over stdio instead.\n- **`dab export --graphql -o ./schema`:** writes `schema.graphql`, which client code generators can use.\n- **Add a new entity** is `dab add` (it has its own question).\n\n**Source:** [dab configure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-configure) · [dab validate](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-validate) · [dab start](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-start) · [dab export](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-export)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`dab configure`","`dab validate`","`dab start`","`dab export --graphql -o ./schema`"],"values":["Add a new entity","Change runtime or data-source settings, never entities, all-or-nothing","Check the config in stages and return a nonzero exit code on failure","Run the DAB engine with a config file","Save the GraphQL schema to a file"],"answer":["Change runtime or data-source settings, never entities, all-or-nothing","Check the config in stages and return a nonzero exit code on failure","Run the DAB engine with a config file","Save the GraphQL schema to a file"]}},{"id":"dab-3.3-2","domain":2,"section":"3","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 3.3 · dab configure, validate, start and export","scenario":"","stem":"`dab validate` fails at its first stage. What happens to the later stages (config properties, permissions, connectivity, metadata)?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Validation runs **in order**: schema, config properties (for example at least one endpoint enabled, REST and GraphQL paths starting with `/` and not colliding, and both issuer and audience set when using JWT), permissions, database connectivity and metadata. If one stage fails, the later ones are skipped. Exit code 0 means every stage passed.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Later stages are skipped:** if the JSON doesn't match the schema (say, `\"permisions\"` misspelled), validation stops there, and connectivity and metadata aren't checked. Fix it, rerun, and the next problem (maybe the database is unreachable) appears.\n- **All errors together** is false. Each stage depends on the previous one: there's no point checking permissions in a file that doesn't parse.\n- **Starts anyway in development mode** is false. `dab validate` never starts the engine.\n- **Only with `--verbose`** is false. `--verbose` adds detail to the output, but it doesn't change which stages run.\n\n**Source:** [dab configure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-configure) · [dab validate](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-validate) · [dab start](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-start) · [dab export](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/command-line/dab-export)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"They're skipped, because validation stops at the first failing stage"},{"key":"B","text":"They still run and report all errors together"},{"key":"C","text":"DAB starts anyway in development mode"},{"key":"D","text":"They're run only with `--verbose`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-4.1-1","domain":1,"section":"4","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 4.1 · Sources: tables, views and stored procedures","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the entity that exposes the stored procedure `dbo.GetBooksByAuthor` over REST with GET only, and as a GraphQL query.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`source.type` is `table`, `view` or `stored-procedure`. `rest.methods` applies **only to stored procedures** (default POST). `graphql.operation` is `query` or `mutation` (default **mutation**). Stored procedures use the **`execute`** action, and the wildcard `*` on a stored procedure expands to just `execute`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`procedure`** and **`sproc`** aren't source types. The three types are `table`, `view` and `stored-procedure`.\n- **`post`** is the default REST method for stored procedures. The requirement is GET only. **`read`** isn't an HTTP method, it's a permission action.\n- **`mutation`** is the default GraphQL operation for stored procedures, which suits procedures that change data. `GetBooksByAuthor` only reads, so `query` fits. **`read`** isn't an operation.\n- **`read`** and **`create`** are actions for tables and views. A stored procedure only supports `execute` (and `*`, which on a procedure means just `execute`).\n\nThe result: `GET /api/BooksByAuthor?author=Pratchett` runs `EXEC dbo.GetBooksByAuthor @author = 'Pratchett'`.\n\n**Source:** [entities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/entities) · [views](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/views) · [stored procedures](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/stored-procedures)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"\"BooksByAuthor\": {\n  \"source\": { \"object\": \"dbo.GetBooksByAuthor\", \"type\": \"[[0]]\" },\n  \"rest\": { \"methods\": [ \"[[1]]\" ] },\n  \"graphql\": { \"operation\": \"[[2]]\" },\n  \"permissions\": [ { \"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [ \"[[3]]\" ] } ]\n}","options":[["procedure","sproc","stored-procedure"],["get","post","read"],["mutation","query","read"],["create","execute","read"]],"answer":["stored-procedure","get","query","execute"]}},{"id":"dab-4.1-2","domain":1,"section":"4","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 4.1 · Sources: tables, views and stored procedures","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about entity sources.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Views need `key-fields` (deprecated in 2.0) or `fields` with `primary-key: true`. Table and view permissions control which verbs work (`methods` is only for stored procedures). The 2.0 `parameters` array has name, description, required and default; the dictionary form still works but is deprecated.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Views need key fields:** without them, `GET /api/BookSummary/id/1` can't work, because DAB has no way of knowing which column identifies a row.\n- **`*` on a table:** `\"actions\": [\"*\"]` for `editor` allows POST, GET, PUT, PATCH and DELETE.\n- **`rest.methods` on a table** is false. It only exists for stored procedures. To make a table read-only, grant only `read`. A POST then returns 403.\n- **Parameters as an array in 2.0:**\n\n  ```json\n  \"parameters\": [ { \"name\": \"author\", \"required\": true, \"description\": \"Author's last name\" } ]\n  ```\n\n  The old form `\"parameters\": { \"author\": \"\" }` still loads, but it's deprecated.\n\n**Source:** [entities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/entities) · [views](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/views) · [stored procedures](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/stored-procedures)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["A view needs key fields defined, because views have no intrinsic primary key.","For a table, the wildcard action `*` means create, read, update and delete.","`rest.methods` can restrict a table entity to GET only.","In DAB 2.0, stored procedure parameters are defined as an array; the old dictionary format is deprecated."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"dab-4.1-3","domain":1,"section":"4","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 4.1 · Sources: tables, views and stored procedures","scenario":"","stem":"You expose a table named `dbo.Books` as the entity `Book` but want GraphQL to use `book` and `books` as the type names. Which setting do you use?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"`graphql.type` sets the names in the GraphQL schema: a string, or an object with `singular` (required) and `plural` (defaults to the singular). `rest.path` customizes the REST route, and `graphql.enabled`/`rest.enabled` turn an endpoint off for one entity. With `dab add`, `--graphql book:books` sets both.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`graphql.type`:** the schema then has `book_by_pk` and `books`, so a query is `{ books { items { title } } }`.\n- **`rest.path: \"/books\"`** changes the REST route to `/api/books`. It doesn't affect GraphQL.\n- **`source.object: \"books\"`** changes which *database object* the entity reads. DAB would look for a table `books` (in the default schema) instead of `dbo.Books`.\n- **`graphql.operation`** only applies to stored procedures, where it picks query or mutation.\n\n**Source:** [entities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/entities) · [views](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/views) · [stored procedures](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/stored-procedures)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`\"graphql\": { \"type\": { \"singular\": \"book\", \"plural\": \"books\" } }`"},{"key":"B","text":"`\"rest\": { \"path\": \"/books\" }`"},{"key":"C","text":"`\"source\": { \"object\": \"books\" }`"},{"key":"D","text":"`\"graphql\": { \"operation\": \"query\" }`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-4.2-1","domain":1,"section":"4","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 4.2 · Fields, aliases and primary keys","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the DAB 2.0 `fields` array that exposes column `book_title` as `title` and marks `id` as the key of a view.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"In DAB 2.0 each `fields` entry has `name` (the database column), and optionally `alias` (the API name), `description` and `primary-key`. It **replaces** the deprecated `mappings` and `source.key-fields`. Descriptions also help MCP agents understand your data.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`column`** and **`label`** aren't `fields` properties. The database column is `name`, and the API name is `alias`.\n- **`key-fields`** is the old `source.key-fields` list, deprecated in 2.0. Each field now has `\"primary-key\": true` instead.\n- **`mappings`** is the old object (`\"mappings\": { \"book_title\": \"title\" }`), also replaced by `fields` with `alias`.\n\nWith this array, `GET /api/BookSummary` returns `{ \"id\": 1, \"title\": \"...\" }`, and MCP agents see the description *\"The book's title\"* when they call `describe_entities`.\n\n**Source:** [entities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/entities)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"\"fields\": [\n  { \"[[0]]\": \"id\", \"[[1]]\": true },\n  { \"name\": \"book_title\", \"[[2]]\": \"title\", \"[[3]]\": \"The book's title\" }\n]","values":["alias","column","description","key-fields","label","mappings","name","primary-key"],"answer":["name","primary-key","alias","description"]}},{"id":"dab-4.3-1","domain":1,"section":"4","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 4.3 · Relationships","scenario":"","stem":"You define a many-to-many relationship between `Book` and `Author` through the `dbo.BookAuthors` join table, without exposing the join table as an entity. Which properties do you use? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"A relationship lives under `relationships.<name>` with `cardinality` (`one` or `many`) and `target.entity`, optionally `source.fields` and `target.fields` (DAB infers them from a foreign key if one exists). **`linking.object`** with `linking.source.fields` and `linking.target.fields` models many-to-many through a join table. Relationships show up in GraphQL as nested fields.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\nOn `Book`:\n\n```json\n\"relationships\": {\n  \"authors\": {\n    \"cardinality\": \"many\",\n    \"target.entity\": \"Author\",\n    \"linking.object\": \"dbo.BookAuthors\",\n    \"linking.source.fields\": [ \"book_id\" ],\n    \"linking.target.fields\": [ \"author_id\" ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nGraphQL then lets a client ask `{ books { items { title authors { items { name } } } } }`, and DAB joins through `dbo.BookAuthors` without it ever being an entity.\n\n- **`rest.methods`** is false. It's a stored-procedure setting, and relationships are a GraphQL feature that REST doesn't use.\n\n**Source:** [relationships](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/graphql/relationships) · [entities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/entities)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`cardinality: \"many\"`"},{"key":"B","text":"`target.entity: \"Author\"`"},{"key":"C","text":"`linking.object: \"dbo.BookAuthors\"`"},{"key":"D","text":"`linking.source.fields` and `linking.target.fields`"},{"key":"E","text":"`rest.methods`"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-4.3-2","domain":1,"section":"4","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 4.3 · Relationships","scenario":"","stem":"`Series` has many `Books`, and the database has a foreign key from `Books.series_id` to `Series.id`. What's the minimum relationship definition on `Series`?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"When a foreign key exists, DAB can infer the joining fields. Specify `source.fields`/`target.fields` when there's no foreign key (relationships can be virtual) and `linking.*` for many-to-many. The relationship appears in GraphQL as a nested field (for example `series { books { items { title } } }`).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Minimum:** `\"relationships\": { \"books\": { \"cardinality\": \"many\", \"target.entity\": \"Book\" } }` on `Series`. DAB finds `Books.series_id → Series.id` from the foreign key, and `{ series { items { name books { items { title } } } } }` works.\n- **Must list the fields** is false while the foreign key exists. Without one (a *virtual* relationship), you'd add `\"source.fields\": [\"id\"], \"target.fields\": [\"series_id\"]`.\n- **A `linking.object`** is only for many-to-many through a join table. One-to-many doesn't need it.\n- **Only in the database** is false. Relationships can be defined in the config even where the database has no foreign key at all.\n\n**Source:** [relationships](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/graphql/relationships) · [entities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/entities)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`cardinality: \"many\"` and `target.entity: \"Book\"`; DAB infers the fields from the foreign key"},{"key":"B","text":"You must list `source.fields` and `target.fields` even with a foreign key"},{"key":"C","text":"A `linking.object` is required"},{"key":"D","text":"Relationships can only be defined in the database, not in the config"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-5.1-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 5.1 · Authentication providers","scenario":"","stem":"Match each scenario to the `runtime.host.authentication.provider` value.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Providers: `Unauthenticated` (default), `EntraId` (or `AzureAD` for compatibility), `Custom` (any JWT issuer), `AppService` (EasyAuth headers), `StaticWebApps` and `Simulator`. Set it with `dab configure --runtime.host.authentication.provider <name>`. JWT providers need `jwt.audience` and `jwt.issuer`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Unauthenticated:** for example, Azure API Management in front of DAB validates tokens and passes only good requests through. DAB treats every request as `anonymous`.\n- **EntraId:** a SPA signs users in with MSAL and calls DAB with `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. DAB validates the token's audience and issuer.\n- **Custom:** the same, but the tokens come from Okta, Auth0 or Keycloak.\n- **AppService:** App Service's built-in authentication signs the user in and passes the identity in the `X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL` header.\n- **Simulator:** `curl -H \"X-MS-API-ROLE: editor\" http://localhost:5000/api/Book` tests the `editor` permissions with no token at all.\n- **`StaticWebApps-Only`** isn't a provider. The Static Web Apps provider is `StaticWebApps`, used when DAB runs as an SWA database connection.\n\n**Source:** [authenticate overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authenticate-overview) · [runtime](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/runtime)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["A trusted gateway in front of DAB already handles identity (the default)","Production app with Microsoft identity tokens","Tokens from Okta, Auth0 or Keycloak","DAB hosted in Azure App Service with built-in authentication","Local permission testing without tokens"],"values":["AppService","Custom","EntraId","Simulator","StaticWebApps-Only","Unauthenticated"],"answer":["Unauthenticated","EntraId","Custom","AppService","Simulator"]}},{"id":"dab-5.2-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 5.2 · Microsoft Entra ID and custom JWT","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the authentication block for Microsoft Entra ID.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The **audience** is your DAB app registration's Application ID URI (for example `api://<app-id>`), and the **issuer** is `https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0`. Clients send `Authorization: Bearer <token>`; a valid token gets the `Authenticated` role unless `X-MS-API-ROLE` picks another role from the token's `roles` claim.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`<tenant-id>`** as audience: the audience names the *API* the token is for, not the tenant. A token's `aud` claim is never just the tenant ID, so every token would be rejected.\n- **`https://graph.microsoft.com`** is the audience of tokens for Microsoft Graph. Accepting them would let any token a user got for Graph also call your API.\n- **`https://management.azure.com`** is Azure Resource Manager's audience, not an issuer.\n- **`https://sts.windows.net`** is the start of the v1.0 issuer (`https://sts.windows.net/<tenant-id>/`). Without the tenant ID, no token's `iss` claim matches it. For v2.0 tokens, the issuer is `https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0`.\n\n**Source:** [authenticate entra](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authenticate-entra) · [authenticate custom](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authenticate-custom)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"\"host\": {\n  \"authentication\": {\n    \"provider\": \"EntraId\",\n    \"jwt\": {\n      \"audience\": \"[[0]]\",\n      \"issuer\": \"[[1]]\"\n    }\n  }\n}","options":[["<tenant-id>","api://<app-id>","https://graph.microsoft.com"],["https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0","https://management.azure.com","https://sts.windows.net"]],"answer":["api://<app-id>","https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0"]}},{"id":"dab-5.2-2","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 5.2 · Microsoft Entra ID and custom JWT","scenario":"","stem":"DAB validates users' Entra ID tokens. By default, which identity does it use to connect to Azure SQL?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"DAB validates the incoming token for API authentication, but connects to the database with **its own** credentials. It doesn't exchange the user's token by default. If the database must see the real caller, enable **On-Behalf-Of** (DAB 2.0, Microsoft SQL with Entra ID only).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Its own identity:** Alice and Bob both call DAB with their own tokens, but in the database's audit log every query comes from `dab-app`, the managed identity in the connection string. Permissions per user are enforced by DAB's roles and policies, not by SQL.\n- **Each caller's identity, automatically** is false. That needs **On-Behalf-Of**, which you have to configure (it has its own question).\n- **The `oid` claim as a SQL login** is false. DAB never logs in as a claim value. It can *pass* claims to SQL with `set-session-context`.\n- **An anonymous database user** doesn't exist in Azure SQL. Every connection authenticates as some login or identity.\n\n**Source:** [authenticate entra](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authenticate-entra) · [authenticate custom](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authenticate-custom)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Its own identity: a managed identity or the connection string's credentials"},{"key":"B","text":"Each calling user's identity, automatically"},{"key":"C","text":"The token's `oid` claim as a SQL login"},{"key":"D","text":"An anonymous database user"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-5.2-3","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 5.2 · Microsoft Entra ID and custom JWT","scenario":"","stem":"You configure the `Custom` provider for Okta. Answer Yes or No for each statement.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`jwt.issuer` is used as the JWT authority, so keys come from `<issuer>/.well-known/openid-configuration`. The role claim type is fixed as **`roles`**. Clients pick a role with `X-MS-API-ROLE`, as with Entra ID.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Issuer, audience, signature:** a token from another Okta org, a token for another API, or a token someone edited all get **401**.\n- **Keys from OIDC metadata:** with `\"issuer\": \"https://contoso.okta.com/oauth2/default\"`, DAB reads `https://contoso.okta.com/oauth2/default/.well-known/openid-configuration` to find the signing keys. When Okta rotates its keys, nothing needs to change in DAB.\n- **Choosing the role claim** is false. DAB always reads roles from the `roles` claim. If Okta puts groups in a `groups` claim, configure Okta to also emit `roles`.\n- **Audience:** for example, `api://default` (Okta's default authorization server), or the client ID, whichever the tokens' `aud` claim contains.\n\n**Source:** [authenticate entra](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authenticate-entra) · [authenticate custom](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authenticate-custom)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["DAB validates the token's issuer, audience and signature.","Signing keys are discovered from the issuer's OpenID Connect metadata.","You can configure which claim DAB reads roles from.","Okta's client ID or API identifier is used as the audience."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"dab-5.3-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 5.3 · Simulator and App Service (EasyAuth)","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about the Simulator and AppService providers.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"**Simulator** is development-only: it treats every request as authenticated without a token, so you can test permissions. **AppService** trusts EasyAuth's `X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL` header; make sure clients can't bypass EasyAuth. Outside App Service the provider causes startup failures because EasyAuth isn't there.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Simulator needs development mode:** with `host.mode: production`, DAB won't start with the Simulator provider, so it can't end up in production by accident.\n- **Authenticated by default:** `curl http://localhost:5000/api/Book` is treated as `Authenticated`, and `-H \"X-MS-API-ROLE: editor\"` makes it `editor`, with no token.\n- **`X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL`:** EasyAuth adds a Base64-encoded JSON header such as `{\"auth_typ\":\"aad\",\"claims\":[{\"typ\":\"roles\",\"val\":\"editor\"}, ...]}`, and DAB reads the identity from it.\n- **A bare Windows server** is false. Without App Service, no EasyAuth sits in front of DAB, so there's no trustworthy `X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL`. A client could forge the header. Use `EntraId` or `Custom` there.\n\n**Source:** [authenticate simulator](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authenticate-simulator) · [authenticate easy auth](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authenticate-easy-auth)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["The Simulator provider requires `host.mode` to be `development`.","With Simulator, requests get the `Authenticated` role unless `X-MS-API-ROLE` asks for another.","The AppService provider reads identity from the Base64-encoded `X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL` header injected by EasyAuth.","The AppService provider is a good choice for a bare Windows server without App Service."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"dab-5.4-1","domain":2,"section":"5","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 5.4 · On-Behalf-Of and the database connection identity","scenario":"","stem":"You enable On-Behalf-Of (OBO) authentication so Azure SQL sees each caller's identity. Which statements are true? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"OBO (pass-through) exchanges the caller's token for a SQL token. Set `user-delegated-auth` on the data source; don't put `Authentication=Active Directory Managed Identity` in the connection string, because SqlClient throws if `AccessToken` is also set. DAB still uses a managed identity token for health checks and internal operations.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n```json\n\"data-source\": {\n  \"database-type\": \"mssql\",\n  \"connection-string\": \"Server=tcp:contoso.database.windows.net;Database=Library;\",\n  \"user-delegated-auth\": { \"enabled\": true, \"provider\": \"EntraId\", \"database-audience\": \"https://database.windows.net\" }\n}\n```\n\n- **No `Authentication=` keyword:** DAB supplies an access token per request, and SqlClient throws if a connection has both an `Authentication` keyword and an `AccessToken`.\n- **SQL with Entra ID only:** OBO exchanges Alice's Entra token for an Azure SQL token for Alice, so `SELECT SUSER_SNAME()` returns `alice@contoso.com`, and row-level security or auditing can use her real identity.\n- **Environment variables:** the app registration that does the exchange is configured with variables such as `DAB_OBO_CLIENT_ID`, not in the config file.\n- **Cosmos DB for NoSQL** is false. OBO is only implemented for Microsoft SQL with Entra ID.\n\n**Source:** [authenticate on behalf of](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authenticate-on-behalf-of) · [data source](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/data-source)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Configure `data-source.user-delegated-auth` with `enabled: true`, provider `EntraId` and the `database-audience`"},{"key":"B","text":"The connection string must not contain an `Authentication=` keyword"},{"key":"C","text":"OBO is supported only for Microsoft SQL with Entra ID"},{"key":"D","text":"DAB reads app registration settings such as `DAB_OBO_CLIENT_ID` from environment variables"},{"key":"E","text":"OBO works with Cosmos DB for NoSQL"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-6.1-1","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 6.1 · System roles, user roles and X-MS-API-ROLE","scenario":"","stem":"Match each request to the effective role DAB uses (JWT provider).","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"The two **system roles** are `Anonymous` and `Authenticated`. To use a **user role**, the token must contain it in `roles` *and* the client must send `X-MS-API-ROLE`. DAB evaluates exactly **one** effective role per request, and no role has permissions by default.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **No `Authorization` header → Anonymous:** `curl https://api.contoso.com/api/Book` is evaluated with the `anonymous` permissions.\n- **Valid token, no header → Authenticated**, even if the token's `roles` claim contains `editor`.\n- **`X-MS-API-ROLE: editor` with `editor` in the token → editor:** only now are the `editor` permissions used, and only those.\n- **`X-MS-API-ROLE: admin` without `admin` in the token → 403:** the token is fine, but it doesn't grant the role asked for.\n- **Invalid token → 401:** for example, an expired token or the wrong audience.\n- **`admin`** as an effective role would need `admin` in the token's `roles` claim.\n\n**Source:** [authorization overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authorization-overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["No `Authorization` header","A valid token, no `X-MS-API-ROLE`","A valid token with `X-MS-API-ROLE: editor`, and `editor` is in the token's `roles` claim","A valid token with `X-MS-API-ROLE: admin`, but `admin` isn't in the token","An invalid token"],"values":["Anonymous","Authenticated","Rejected with 401 Unauthorized","Rejected with 403 Forbidden","admin","editor"],"answer":["Anonymous","Authenticated","editor","Rejected with 403 Forbidden","Rejected with 401 Unauthorized"]}},{"id":"dab-6.1-2","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 6.1 · System roles, user roles and X-MS-API-ROLE","scenario":"","stem":"Which header does a client send to have its request evaluated as the user role `author`?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"`X-MS-API-ROLE` selects one role from the token's `roles` claim. `X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL` is the identity header that App Service EasyAuth injects; it isn't how a client picks a role.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`X-MS-API-ROLE: author`:** sent with a token whose `roles` claim includes `author`.\n- **`X-Role`** isn't a header DAB reads, so the request is evaluated as `Authenticated`.\n- **`Authorization: Role author`** isn't a valid authorization scheme. DAB expects `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, so this request has no valid token: it's rejected, or treated as anonymous.\n- **`X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL`** is the identity header App Service's EasyAuth adds (Base64 JSON). A client doesn't send it, and a bare value such as `author` isn't valid there.\n\n**Source:** [authorization overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/authorization-overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`X-MS-API-ROLE: author`"},{"key":"B","text":"`X-Role: author`"},{"key":"C","text":"`Authorization: Role author`"},{"key":"D","text":"`X-MS-CLIENT-PRINCIPAL: author`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-6.2-1","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 6.2 · Permissions, actions and field access","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the permission so the `auditor` role can read `User` rows, sees every column except `last_login`, and only gets non-admin users.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Actions are `create`, `read`, `update`, `delete`, `execute` and `*`. `fields.include`/`exclude` limit columns (`*` = all; exclude wins). A **database policy** is an OData-style predicate that references fields with `@item.<field>` and claims with `@claims.<type>`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`get`** and **`select`** aren't actions. The actions are `create`, `read`, `update`, `delete`, `execute` and `*`.\n- **`%`** and **`all`** aren't field wildcards. Only `*` means every field.\n- **`deny`** and **`hidden`** aren't keys. Only `include` and `exclude`, and `exclude` wins when a column is in both.\n- **`@claims.IsAdmin eq false`** would test a claim *in the caller's token*, not the row. It filters nothing per row and gives 403 when the token has no such claim.\n- **`IsAdmin = 0`** is T-SQL. Policies are written in OData style (`eq`, `ne`, `and`), with `@item.` before field names.\n\nWith the right answer, `GET /api/User` runs with `WHERE [IsAdmin] = 0` added and returns no `last_login` column.\n\n**Source:** [entities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/entities)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"\"permissions\": [ {\n  \"role\": \"auditor\",\n  \"actions\": [ {\n    \"action\": \"[[0]]\",\n    \"fields\": { \"include\": [ \"[[1]]\" ], \"[[2]]\": [ \"last_login\" ] },\n    \"policy\": { \"database\": \"[[3]]\" }\n  } ]\n} ]","options":[["get","read","select"],["%","*","all"],["deny","exclude","hidden"],["@claims.IsAdmin eq false","@item.IsAdmin eq false","IsAdmin = 0"]],"answer":["read","*","exclude","@item.IsAdmin eq false"]}},{"id":"dab-6.2-2","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 6.2 · Permissions, actions and field access","scenario":"","stem":"Which statements about permissions are true? Each correct answer is a complete statement.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Permissions are an array of `{ role, actions }`. Each action can be a string or an object with `fields` and `policy`. Each action is separate: `read` doesn't imply any write. Use the most restrictive set of roles and fields that works.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Nothing until granted:** an entity with `\"permissions\": [ { \"role\": \"editor\", \"actions\": [\"read\"] } ]` returns 403 to anonymous callers, and to authenticated callers that don't pick `editor`.\n- **Anonymous access:** `{ \"role\": \"anonymous\", \"actions\": [\"read\"] }` lets anyone, with or without a token, read the entity.\n- **`*` then exclude:** `\"fields\": { \"include\": [\"*\"], \"exclude\": [\"ssn\", \"salary\"] }` exposes every column except those two, including columns added later.\n- **`read` implies `update`** is false. Each action is granted separately. A role with only `read` gets **403** on PATCH.\n\n**Source:** [entities](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/entities)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"No role has any permission until the entity's `permissions` grant actions to it"},{"key":"B","text":"To allow unauthenticated access, grant actions to the `anonymous` role"},{"key":"C","text":"Field rules can include `*` and then exclude specific columns"},{"key":"D","text":"Granting `read` also allows `update`"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-6.3-1","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 6.3 · Role inheritance","scenario":"","stem":"In DAB 2.0, an entity has permissions only for `anonymous` (read). A request arrives with the named role `editor`, which has no permission block. What happens?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"**Role inheritance**: named role → `authenticated` → `anonymous`. DAB walks up the chain to the first role with a permission block; if even `anonymous` has none, it returns 403. Inheritance never combines permissions from several roles.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Falls back to `anonymous`:** `editor` has no block, and neither does `authenticated`, so DAB uses `anonymous`, and the editor can read.\n- **403 because `editor` has no block** was the behaviour before role inheritance. In 2.0, you'd only get 403 if even `anonymous` had no permissions.\n- **Every action because named roles are trusted** is false. A role only ever gets what some block in its chain grants.\n- **Combining all roles in the token** is false. DAB always evaluates exactly one role per request. Inheritance just decides *which* block that role uses.\n\n**Source:** [role inheritance](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/role-inheritance)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`editor` inherits from `authenticated`, which isn't configured, so it falls back to `anonymous` and can read"},{"key":"B","text":"The request is rejected with 403 because `editor` has no block"},{"key":"C","text":"`editor` gets every action, because named roles are trusted"},{"key":"D","text":"DAB combines the permissions of all roles in the token"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-6.4-1","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 6.4 · Database policies","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about database policies.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"DAB turns the policy into a `WHERE` predicate, so it works for read, update and delete, but **not create** (INSERT has no WHERE) or **execute** (stored procedures). Operators include `eq`, `ne`, `gt`, `ge`, `lt`, `le`, `and` and `or`; use mapped field names. Cosmos DB for NoSQL doesn't support them.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\nFor example, `\"policy\": { \"database\": \"@item.owner_id eq @claims.oid\" }` on `Note`:\n\n- **read, update, delete:** `GET /api/Note` returns only the caller's notes, and `DELETE /api/Note/id/7` deletes nothing (404) if note 7 belongs to someone else. DAB adds `WHERE [owner_id] = @claim_oid` to each statement.\n- **`create`** is false. An `INSERT` has no `WHERE` to add the predicate to, so DAB doesn't support a database policy on `create`. Enforce ownership on insert with a database constraint, a trigger, or by setting `owner_id` from `SESSION_CONTEXT`.\n- **Missing claim → 403:** a token without `oid` can't be evaluated safely, so DAB refuses the request.\n- **Cosmos DB for NoSQL** is false: database policies are SQL-only.\n\n**Source:** [database policies](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/database-policies)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["They apply to the `read`, `update` and `delete` actions.","They apply to the `create` action.","If a claim used in the policy is missing from the token, the request is rejected with 403.","Azure Cosmos DB for NoSQL supports database policies."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"dab-6.5-1","domain":2,"section":"6","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 6.5 · Session context and row-level security","scenario":"","stem":"You want SQL Server row-level security to filter rows by the caller's role in DAB. Which statements are true? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"With `set-session-context` (mssql only), DAB sends claims such as `roles` through `sp_set_session_context`, and a security policy's predicate can compare against `SESSION_CONTEXT`. Because results now depend on the user, DAB **disables caching** for that data source. Consider indexing the predicate column, or use DAB database policies when they're enough.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n```sql\nCREATE FUNCTION Security.fn_RegionFilter (@Region nvarchar(20))\nRETURNS TABLE WITH SCHEMABINDING\nAS RETURN SELECT 1 AS ok\n   WHERE @Region = CAST(SESSION_CONTEXT(N'region') AS nvarchar(20))\n      OR CAST(SESSION_CONTEXT(N'roles') AS nvarchar(200)) LIKE N'%admin%';\nGO\nCREATE SECURITY POLICY Security.RegionPolicy\n  ADD FILTER PREDICATE Security.fn_RegionFilter(Region) ON dbo.Orders WITH (STATE = ON);\n```\n\n- **`set-session-context`:** before each query, DAB calls `sp_set_session_context` for the token's claims, so `region` and `roles` are there for the function to read.\n- **Caching off:** two users with different regions must never get each other's cached results, so DAB turns caching off for that data source.\n- **Every database type** is false. `SESSION_CONTEXT` is a SQL Server feature, so this option is `mssql` only.\n\n**Source:** [row level security](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/security/row-level-security)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Enable `set-session-context` so DAB calls `sp_set_session_context` with the user's claims"},{"key":"B","text":"The RLS predicate function can read `SESSION_CONTEXT(N'roles')`"},{"key":"C","text":"Enabling session context disables response caching for that data source"},{"key":"D","text":"Session context works with every database type DAB supports"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-7.1-1","domain":1,"section":"7","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 7.1 · Routes, verbs and responses","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about DAB's REST endpoint.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Records are addressed as `/api/{entity}/{pk-column}/{pk-value}`. Responses are a JSON object with a `value` array (plus `nextLink` when there's another page). A missing key returns **200 OK with an empty `value` array**. Everything in the path and query string is case sensitive.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`/api/{entity}`:** `GET /api/Book` returns `{ \"value\": [ { \"id\": 1010, \"title\": \"...\" }, ... ], \"nextLink\": \"...\" }`.\n- **Key in the path:** `GET /api/Book/id/1010`. A composite key repeats the pattern: `/api/OrderLine/order_id/5/line_no/2`.\n- **404 for a missing key** is false. `GET /api/Book/id/99999` returns **200 OK** with `{ \"value\": [] }`, so a client checks for an empty array, not a 404.\n- **Case sensitive:** `GET /api/book` fails if the entity is `Book`, and `$Filter` isn't `$filter`.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["The default route is `/api/{entity}`.","`GET /api/book/id/1010` returns the book whose primary key `id` is 1010.","A GET for a primary key that doesn't exist returns 404 Not Found.","Path segments and query parameter names are case sensitive."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"dab-7.1-2","domain":1,"section":"7","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 7.1 · Routes, verbs and responses","scenario":"","stem":"Match each REST verb to its default behaviour in DAB.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"PUT and PATCH are **upserts** by default. Inserting through them only works when the database accepts explicit key values; when all keys are autogenerated (for example `IDENTITY`), you can send keyless PUT/PATCH and DAB assigns the key.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **POST:** `POST /api/Book` with `{ \"title\": \"Dune\", \"year\": 1965 }` → 201 Created.\n- **PUT:** `PUT /api/Book/id/7` with the whole row replaces it, so send every column you want to keep. If row 7 doesn't exist, it's inserted.\n- **PATCH:** `PATCH /api/Book/id/7` with `{ \"year\": 1966 }` changes only `year`.\n- **DELETE:** `DELETE /api/Book/id/7` → 204 No Content.\n- **Return the item's history** isn't something any verb does. DAB has no history or versioning; with a temporal table, you'd expose the history table or a view over it as its own entity.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["POST","PUT","PATCH","DELETE"],"values":["Create a new item","Remove the item by primary key","Replace the item, or insert it if missing","Return the item's history","Update the given fields, or insert if missing"],"answer":["Create a new item","Replace the item, or insert it if missing","Update the given fields, or insert if missing","Remove the item by primary key"]}},{"id":"dab-7.1-3","domain":1,"section":"7","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 7.1 · Routes, verbs and responses","scenario":"","stem":"A .NET client sends extra properties in a POST body. With the default settings, what does DAB do?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"`runtime.rest.request-body-strict` is true by default, so unknown fields cause a BadRequest. Setting it to false makes DAB ignore extra fields, so one POCO class can be reused for every verb. Computed and autogenerated columns are always ignored.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **BadRequest:** posting `{ \"title\": \"Dune\", \"isFavorite\": true }` to `Book`, which has no `isFavorite` column, returns 400 with a message naming the unknown field.\n- **Ignores the extra properties** is what happens with `\"request-body-strict\": false`. That's convenient when one C# class carries computed properties, but typos then fail silently.\n- **Creates new columns** is false. DAB never changes the schema.\n- **Stores them in a JSON column** is false. There's no catch-all column. Extra properties are rejected, or ignored with strict mode off.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/overview)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Rejects the request with BadRequest, because `request-body-strict` defaults to true"},{"key":"B","text":"Ignores the extra properties"},{"key":"C","text":"Creates new columns for them"},{"key":"D","text":"Stores them in a JSON column"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-7.2-1","domain":1,"section":"7","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 7.2 · Query keywords: $select, $filter, $orderby, $first, $after","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the request: published books over 300 pages, only `title` and `year`, newest first, 10 per page.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"REST keywords are `$select`, `$filter` (OData operators `eq`, `ne`, `gt`, `ge`, `lt`, `le`, `and`, `or`), `$orderby` (`asc`/`desc`, default primary key ascending), `$first` and `$after`. Omitting `$select` returns every field the role can read; there's no `*` token.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`$where`** and **`filter`** (without `$`) aren't REST keywords. DAB would treat `filter` as an unknown query parameter.\n- **`$fields`** and **`select`** aren't keywords either. `$select=title,year` is the one.\n- **`$sort`** doesn't exist, and **`orderBy`** is the *GraphQL* argument (`books(orderBy: { year: DESC })`), not REST.\n- **`$limit`** and **`$top`** aren't supported. OData clients expecting `$top` and `$skip` have to use `$first` and the `$after` cursor.\n\n**Source:** [filter rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/filter-rest) · [orderby rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/orderby-rest) · [after rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/after-rest) · [select rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/select-rest)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"template":"GET /api/book?[[0]]=pages gt 300 and status eq 'published'\n             &[[1]]=title,year\n             &[[2]]=year desc\n             &[[3]]=10","options":[["$filter","$where","filter"],["$fields","$select","select"],["$orderby","$sort","orderBy"],["$first","$limit","$top"]],"answer":["$filter","$select","$orderby","$first"]}},{"id":"dab-7.2-2","domain":1,"section":"7","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 7.2 · Query keywords: $select, $filter, $orderby, $first, $after","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about REST paging.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`runtime.pagination.default-page-size` defaults to 100 and `max-page-size` to 100,000. Paging is cursor-based: follow `nextLink`, whose **`$after`** value is an opaque token, not an offset. `$first=-1` asks for the configured maximum.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **100 by default:** `GET /api/Book` on a table with 250 rows returns 100, plus a `nextLink`.\n- **`nextLink`:** for example, `\"nextLink\": \"https://.../api/Book?$after=W3siRW50aXR5TmFtZSI6...\"`. Call it as is to get rows 101–200.\n- **`$first=-1`:** returns up to `max-page-size` (100,000 by default) in one response. Use it carefully on big tables.\n- **`$after` as a row count** is false. `$after=100` isn't valid. It's an opaque cursor that encodes the last row's sort values, so pages stay correct even if rows are inserted between calls.\n\n**Source:** [filter rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/filter-rest) · [orderby rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/orderby-rest) · [after rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/after-rest) · [select rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/select-rest)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["By default, DAB returns up to 100 items.","The `nextLink` in a response contains an opaque `$after` continuation token.","`$first=-1` requests the configured maximum page size.","`$after` is the number of rows to skip."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"dab-7.2-3","domain":1,"section":"7","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 7.2 · Query keywords: $select, $filter, $orderby, $first, $after","scenario":"","stem":"You call `GET /api/book?$orderby=year desc` and several books share a year. How does DAB break the tie?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The default order is primary key ascending, and DAB appends the primary key fields as tie-breakers so cursor paging with `$after` stays stable. With a composite key, it uses the database column order.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Appends the key:** the query DAB runs is `ORDER BY [year] DESC, [id] ASC`. Two books from 2011 always come in the same order, so `$after` can continue exactly where the last page ended.\n- **Randomly:** an unstable sort is exactly what would break cursor paging, because a page boundary could skip or repeat rows.\n- **By insertion time:** tables have no insertion time unless you add a column, and DAB doesn't sort by one.\n- **An error:** duplicate sort values are normal, and the key makes the order unique.\n\n**Source:** [filter rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/filter-rest) · [orderby rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/orderby-rest) · [after rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/after-rest) · [select rest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/select-rest)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It appends the remaining primary key columns to the sort automatically"},{"key":"B","text":"Randomly"},{"key":"C","text":"By insertion time"},{"key":"D","text":"It returns an error"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-7.3-1","domain":1,"section":"7","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 7.3 · Upserts, If-Match and Location","scenario":"","stem":"Match each request to the status code DAB returns.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Without `If-Match`, PUT/PATCH upsert (201 if they insert, though `Location` might be omitted). **`If-Match: *`** turns them into update-only: missing rows return 404. DAB doesn't support ETags, so any other `If-Match` value is a 400.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **201 Created:** `POST /api/Book` returns the new row, with a `Location` header holding the path to its key (`.../id/1011`), so the client knows where it is.\n- **200 OK:** `PATCH /api/Book/id/7` with `{ \"year\": 1966 }` when row 7 exists.\n- **404 with `If-Match: *`:** `If-Match: *` means *only if it exists*, so the PATCH won't insert a missing row 99. Use it when an accidental insert would be a bug.\n- **400 for `If-Match: \"abc123\"`:** DAB has no ETags, so it can't check a specific version and rejects the request rather than ignoring the condition.\n- **409 Conflict** isn't the result of any of these four requests.\n\n**Source:** [http if match](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/http-if-match) · [http location](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/http-location)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["POST that creates a row","PUT or PATCH that updates an existing row","PATCH with `If-Match: *` for a row that doesn't exist","PUT with `If-Match: \"abc123\"`"],"values":["200 OK","201 Created, with a Location header for the new key","400 Bad Request (only `*` is supported)","404 Not Found","409 Conflict"],"answer":["201 Created, with a Location header for the new key","200 OK","404 Not Found","400 Bad Request (only `*` is supported)"]}},{"id":"dab-7.4-1","domain":1,"section":"7","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 7.4 · OpenAPI and Swagger","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about OpenAPI in DAB 2.0.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"DAB builds an **OpenAPI 3.0.1** document from the config and database metadata. In 2.0 it's **permission-aware**: if `anonymous` can only read `Book`, its document shows only GET for `/api/Book`, and excluded fields are hidden. Development mode also enables Swagger UI and Nitro.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`/api/openapi`:** a client generator such as `npx @openapitools/openapi-generator-cli generate -i http://localhost:5000/api/openapi -g typescript-fetch` can build a typed client from it. If the REST path is `/rest`, the document is at `/rest/openapi`.\n- **Role-aware:** if `anonymous` can only read `Book` and the `cost` field is excluded, its document has only `GET /api/Book`, and no `cost` property in the schema.\n- **Swagger UI:** in development mode, `/swagger` renders the same document as a page where you can try each call.\n- **OpenAPI 2.0** is false. The document says `\"openapi\": \"3.0.1\"`, so tools that only read Swagger 2.0 documents need a converter.\n\n**Source:** [openapi](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/rest/openapi)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["The OpenAPI document is available at `/{rest-path}/openapi`, for example `/api/openapi`.","In 2.0 it shows only the methods and fields a role can use.","Swagger UI is available in development mode.","The document follows OpenAPI 2.0 (Swagger 2.0)."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"dab-8.1-1","domain":1,"section":"8","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 8.1 · Queries and mutations","scenario":"","stem":"Complete the GraphQL requests for a `Book` entity (plural `books`).","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Every GraphQL request is a POST to `/graphql`. Collections return their rows under **`items`** (with `hasNextPage` and `endCursor`), one row by key uses **`<entity>_by_pk`**, and mutations are `createBook`, `updateBook` and `deleteBook`. The `_by_pk` suffix applies only to queries. Mutations need connection pooling (no `Pooling=False`).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`book`** (the singular) isn't a query field. Lists use the plural, `books`, and one row by key uses `book_by_pk`.\n- **`nodes`** is the Relay or Hot Chocolate convention in other GraphQL servers. DAB returns rows under `items`, next to `hasNextPage` and `endCursor`.\n- **`bookById`** isn't generated. DAB's naming is always `<singular>_by_pk`.\n- **`insertBook`** is other GraphQL servers' naming. DAB generates `createBook`, `updateBook` and `deleteBook`.\n- **`createBook_by_pk`** doesn't exist: the `_by_pk` suffix is only for queries.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/graphql/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"template":"# list books\n{ [[0]] { [[1]] { id title } } }\n\n# one book by primary key\n{ [[2]](id: 1010) { id title } }\n\n# create a book\nmutation { [[3]](item: { title: \"Leviathan Wakes\", year: 2011 }) { id title } }","values":["book","bookById","book_by_pk","books","createBook","createBook_by_pk","insertBook","items","nodes"],"answer":["books","items","book_by_pk","createBook"]}},{"id":"dab-8.2-1","domain":1,"section":"8","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 8.2 · Filters, ordering and paging","scenario":"","stem":"Match each GraphQL filter operator to its meaning.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"GraphQL filters differ from REST: `eq`, `neq`, `gt`, `gte`, `lt`, `lte`, `and`, `or`, `contains`, `notContains`, `startsWith`, `endsWith`, `in` and `isNull`. Paging uses `first` and `after` (pass the previous `endCursor`), and sorting uses `orderBy`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n```graphql\n{\n  books(filter: { title: { contains: \"Expanse\" }, year: { gte: 2011 }, isbn: { isNull: false },\n                  status: { neq: \"retired\" } }) {\n    items { title year }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- **`neq`, `gte`, `contains`, `isNull`:** in GraphQL these are spelled differently from REST's OData-style `ne` and `ge`. `contains` becomes `LIKE '%Expanse%'`.\n- **Starts with** is **`startsWith`**, for example `title: { startsWith: \"The\" }`, which becomes `LIKE 'The%'`. `endsWith` and `notContains` also exist.\n\n**Source:** [filter graphql](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/keywords/filter-graphql) · [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/graphql/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`neq`","`gte`","`contains`","`isNull`"],"values":["Greater than or equal","Not equal","Null check","Starts with","Substring match"],"answer":["Not equal","Greater than or equal","Substring match","Null check"]}},{"id":"dab-8.3-1","domain":1,"section":"8","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 8.3 · Aggregation and multiple mutations","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about GraphQL aggregation and multiple mutations.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Aggregation is on by default (turn it off with `runtime.graphql.enable-aggregation: false`) but isn't available for Cosmos DB for NoSQL, PostgreSQL or MySQL; it returns 100 groups per page by default. **Multiple mutations** run in a single transaction and must be enabled with `runtime.graphql.multiple-mutations.create.enabled: true`.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Aggregation on SQL Server:**\n\n  ```graphql\n  {\n    books(orderBy: { year: ASC }) {\n      groupBy(fields: [year]) {\n        fields { year }\n        aggregations { totalPages: sum(field: pages)  averagePages: avg(field: pages) }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  ```\n\n  DAB turns it into a `GROUP BY year` query with `SUM` and `AVG`.\n- **PostgreSQL and MySQL** is false. Aggregation isn't available there (or on Cosmos DB for NoSQL), so `groupBy` isn't in their schemas.\n- **Multiple create in one transaction:** `createBook(item: { title: \"Intro to DAB\", chapters: [ { name: \"Configuration\" }, { name: \"Running\" } ] })` inserts the book and both chapters, or nothing if a chapter fails.\n- **On by default** is false. You need `\"runtime\": { \"graphql\": { \"multiple-mutations\": { \"create\": { \"enabled\": true } } } }`.\n\n**Source:** [aggregate data](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/graphql/aggregate-data) · [multiple mutations](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/graphql/multiple-mutations)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Aggregation (`groupBy` with functions such as sum, avg, min, max) works for SQL Server.","Aggregation is available for PostgreSQL and MySQL.","Multiple-create mutations insert related items, such as a book and its chapters, in one transaction.","Multiple-create mutations are enabled by default."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"dab-8.4-1","domain":1,"section":"8","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 8.4 · Stored procedures in GraphQL","scenario":"","stem":"The stored procedure entity `GetCowrittenBooksByAuthor` is exposed in GraphQL. What's the generated field name?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Stored procedure fields are prefixed with **`execute`**. They're mutations unless `graphql.operation` is `query`, and the role needs the `execute` action. Stored procedures are also excluded from entity health checks, because they need parameters and might not be deterministic.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`executeGetCowrittenBooksByAuthor`:** `mutation { executeGetCowrittenBooksByAuthor(author: \"Pratchett\") { title } }`, or a plain query `{ executeGetCowrittenBooksByAuthor(author: \"Pratchett\") { title } }` if `graphql.operation` is `query`.\n- **`getCowrittenBooksByAuthor_by_pk`:** `_by_pk` fields are for reading one table or view row by primary key. A procedure has no primary key.\n- **`GetCowrittenBooksByAuthorQuery`** and **`runGetCowrittenBooksByAuthor`** aren't DAB's naming. The prefix is always `execute`.\n\n**Source:** [stored procedures](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/graphql/stored-procedures)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`executeGetCowrittenBooksByAuthor`"},{"key":"B","text":"`getCowrittenBooksByAuthor_by_pk`"},{"key":"C","text":"`GetCowrittenBooksByAuthorQuery`"},{"key":"D","text":"`runGetCowrittenBooksByAuthor`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-9.1-1","domain":3,"section":"9","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 9.1 · What SQL MCP Server is","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about SQL MCP Server.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"SQL MCP Server exposes DAB entities as a small family of MCP tools over streamable HTTP (`/mcp`) or stdio. It **intentionally doesn't do NL2SQL**: it uses the entity abstraction and DAB's deterministic query builder (\"NL2DAB\"). It's built around **DML** only, and DAB's RBAC applies at every step.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Part of DAB 1.7+:** it's the same `dab-config.json` and the same `dab start`. MCP is one more endpoint next to REST and GraphQL.\n- **NL2SQL** is false. An agent asking *\"books after 2020\"* calls `read_records` with `{\"entity\": \"Book\", \"filter\": \"year gt 2020\"}`, and DAB generates the T-SQL. The model never writes SQL.\n- **DDL such as creating tables** is false. There's no tool for `CREATE TABLE`, `ALTER` or `DROP`. An agent can only work with rows of entities you configured.\n- **Role-aware:** if the agent's role only has `read` on `Book`, `describe_entities` shows `Book` as read-only, and `create_record` on `Book` is refused.\n\n**Source:** [overview](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/mcp/overview)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["SQL MCP Server is part of Data API builder, from version 1.7.","It generates free-form SQL from natural language (NL2SQL).","It supports DDL operations such as creating tables.","It's role-aware: agents only see entities and operations their role allows."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","No","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"dab-9.2-1","domain":3,"section":"9","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 9.2 · DML tools","scenario":"","stem":"Which tools does SQL MCP Server 2.0 expose by default? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The DML tools are `describe_entities`, `create_record`, `read_records`, `update_record`, `delete_record`, `execute_entity` and (new in 2.0) `aggregate_records`. Agents call `describe_entities` first, then use the entity options it returns. Each tool can be turned off under `runtime.mcp.dml-tools`, and entities opt out with `mcp.dml-tools: false`. There's no raw SQL tool.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`describe_entities`:** the agent's first call. It returns each entity, its fields with descriptions, and which operations the role may use.\n- **`read_records`:** for example `{\"entity\": \"Book\", \"select\": \"title,year\", \"filter\": \"year gt 2020\", \"orderby\": \"year desc\", \"first\": 10}`.\n- **`create_record`:** `{\"entity\": \"Book\", \"data\": {\"title\": \"Dune\", \"year\": 1965}}`.\n- **`aggregate_records`** (new in 2.0): totals and averages grouped by a field, without fetching every row.\n- **`execute_sql`** doesn't exist, on purpose: a tool that runs arbitrary SQL would bypass the entity layer and its permissions. The full set is the seven DML tools.\n\nTurn one off with `\"runtime\": { \"mcp\": { \"dml-tools\": { \"delete-record\": false } } }`.\n\n**Source:** [data manipulation language tools](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/mcp/data-manipulation-language-tools) · [runtime](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/runtime)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`describe_entities`"},{"key":"B","text":"`read_records`"},{"key":"C","text":"`create_record`"},{"key":"D","text":"`aggregate_records`"},{"key":"E","text":"`execute_sql`"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-9.3-1","domain":3,"section":"9","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 9.3 · Custom tools, descriptions and stdio","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about MCP customization.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Custom tools are only for stored-procedure entities (setting `custom-tool` on a table or view is a startup error), and their names are converted to **snake_case**. Entity, field and parameter **descriptions** improve agent accuracy. The stdio transport defaults to `anonymous` when no role is given and uses the Simulator provider.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`mcp.custom-tool: true`:** a stored procedure entity `GetProductById` shows up as its own tool, with its parameters as tool arguments, so the agent doesn't have to go through `execute_entity`.\n- **snake_case:** `GetProductById` becomes `get_product_by_id`, the naming most agents expect.\n- **`custom-tool` on a table** is false. It's only valid on stored-procedure entities, and on a table or view DAB fails at startup. Tables are reached through the generic DML tools.\n- **`--mcp-stdio role:reader`:** an agent that launches DAB itself (such as Claude Desktop or VS Code with a stdio server) gets the `reader` permissions. Without `role:`, it's `anonymous`.\n\n**Source:** [how to configure custom tools](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/mcp/how-to-configure-custom-tools) · [stdio transport](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/mcp/stdio-transport) · [how to add descriptions](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/mcp/how-to-add-descriptions)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["`mcp.custom-tool: true` registers a stored procedure entity as its own named MCP tool.","An entity named `GetProductById` appears as the tool `get_product_by_id`.","`custom-tool` can be set on a table entity.","`dab start --mcp-stdio role:reader` runs the server over stdio in the `reader` role."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"dab-10.1-1","domain":2,"section":"10","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 10.1 · Host mode, paths and pagination","scenario":"","stem":"Match each runtime setting to its default.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Development mode enables Nitro (GraphQL) and Swagger UI, anonymous health checks and Debug logging. Other defaults: `max-page-size` 100,000, `request-body-strict` true (extra fields return BadRequest), `graphql.allow-introspection` true, `depth-limit` unlimited, and `max-response-size-mb` 158.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`/api`, `/graphql`, `/mcp`:** with defaults, `GET http://localhost:5000/api/Book`, `POST http://localhost:5000/graphql` and the MCP endpoint `http://localhost:5000/mcp`.\n- **`production`:** a fresh config is in production mode unless `dab init --host-mode development` is used, so Swagger, Nitro and detailed error messages are off.\n- **100:** `GET /api/Book` returns at most 100 rows per page.\n- **`development`** is the other host mode, not a default. It turns on Swagger UI and Nitro, anonymous health checks and Debug logging, which you want locally and not on the internet.\n\n**Source:** [runtime](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/runtime)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["`runtime.rest.path`","`runtime.graphql.path`","`runtime.mcp.path`","`runtime.host.mode`","`runtime.pagination.default-page-size`"],"values":["/api","/graphql","/mcp","100","development","production"],"answer":["/api","/graphql","/mcp","production","100"]}},{"id":"dab-10.1-2","domain":2,"section":"10","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 10.1 · Host mode, paths and pagination","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each runtime host setting.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"CORS uses `origins` and `allow-credentials` (default false). HTTP compression defaults to `optimal`. Turning introspection off hides the schema, and **`depth-limit`** (unlimited by default) caps how deeply nested a query can be, which helps against expensive queries.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **CORS origins:** `\"cors\": { \"origins\": [\"https://app.contoso.com\"], \"allow-credentials\": false }`. A page on any other origin gets blocked by the browser.\n- **Compression:** `\"compression\": { \"level\": \"fastest\" }` trades a little size for less CPU. `none` turns it off.\n- **Introspection off:** in production, `\"allow-introspection\": false` makes `{ __schema { types { name } } }` fail, so outsiders can't list your types, while normal queries still work.\n- **`depth-limit` defaults to 5** is false. It's unlimited by default. `\"depth-limit\": 5` would reject a query like `books → authors → books → authors → books → authors`, which could otherwise get very expensive.\n\n**Source:** [runtime](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/runtime)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["`runtime.host.cors.origins` lists the browser origins allowed to call DAB.","`runtime.compression.level` can be `optimal`, `fastest` or `none`.","`runtime.graphql.allow-introspection: false` hides the GraphQL schema from introspection.","`runtime.graphql.depth-limit` defaults to 5."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","No"]}},{"id":"dab-10.2-1","domain":2,"section":"10","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 10.2 · Response caching","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about response caching.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Turn on `runtime.cache.enabled` (default TTL 5 seconds), then opt each entity in with `cache.enabled` (TTL inherits if not set). `L1` is in-memory; `L1L2` adds the distributed `level-2` cache (`provider: redis`, `connection-string`, optional `partition`). Request headers: `no-cache` bypasses and refreshes, `no-store` skips caching entirely; they apply to REST queries only.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Global and per entity:**\n\n  ```json\n  \"runtime\": { \"cache\": { \"enabled\": true, \"ttl-seconds\": 30 } },\n  \"entities\": { \"Book\": { \"cache\": { \"enabled\": true } } }\n  ```\n\n  With only one of the two, nothing is cached.\n- **`L1L2` default:** each instance keeps results in memory (L1) and, if a level-2 cache is configured, in Redis as well.\n- **Redis level 2:** with `\"level-2\": { \"enabled\": true, \"provider\": \"redis\", \"connection-string\": \"@env('REDIS')\" }`, three DAB replicas share one cache, so a result cached by one replica serves the other two.\n- **`Cache-Control: no-cache`:** `curl -H \"Cache-Control: no-cache\" /api/Book` skips the cached copy, queries the database, and stores the fresh result. `no-store` neither reads nor writes the cache.\n\n**Source:** [level 1](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/cache/level-1) · [level 2](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/cache/level-2) · [http headers](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/cache/http-headers)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Caching must be enabled globally under `runtime.cache` and also per entity.","An entity's cache `level` defaults to `L1L2`.","Level 2 cache currently uses Redis, so scaled-out DAB instances share cached results.","A REST request with `Cache-Control: no-cache` bypasses the cache, fetches fresh data and refreshes the cache."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"dab-10.2-2","domain":2,"section":"10","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 10.2 · Response caching","scenario":"","stem":"An entity has `\"cache\": { \"enabled\": true }` but no `ttl-seconds`, and the global cache TTL is 30. How long are its results cached?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"Entity TTL inherits from the global setting when not set (the global default is 5 seconds). Caching is off unless both the runtime and the entity enable it.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **30 seconds:** the entity inherits `runtime.cache.ttl-seconds`. Override per entity with `\"cache\": { \"enabled\": true, \"ttl-seconds\": 300 }` for data that rarely changes.\n- **5 seconds** is the global default, used only when `runtime.cache.ttl-seconds` isn't set. Here it's set to 30.\n- **Forever** is false. Every cached result has a TTL.\n- **Not cached** is false. Both the runtime and the entity enable caching, so results are cached.\n\n**Source:** [level 1](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/cache/level-1) · [level 2](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/cache/level-2) · [http headers](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/cache/http-headers)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"30 seconds, inherited from `runtime.cache.ttl-seconds`"},{"key":"B","text":"5 seconds"},{"key":"C","text":"Forever, until restart"},{"key":"D","text":"They aren't cached"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-10.3-1","domain":2,"section":"10","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 10.3 · Health checks","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about the `/health` endpoint.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"Health checks report `Healthy` or not, per data source (with an optional `name` to tell sources apart) and per entity (`first` 1–500). Stored procedures are skipped. Results are cached for `cache-ttl-seconds` (default 5) and run with `max-query-parallelism` (default 4). Development mode allows anonymous health checks.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **Data source check:** `GET /health` returns an overall status plus one check per data source, with its response time compared against `threshold-ms`. A database that answers in 2,500 ms with the default 1,000 ms threshold shows as unhealthy, so a load balancer's probe can take that instance out of rotation.\n- **Entity check:** each REST- or GraphQL-enabled entity is read (`first` rows, 100 by default), which also catches a dropped table or a broken permission.\n- **Stored procedures included** is false. They need parameters, and running one could change data, so health checks skip them.\n- **`health.roles` in production:** with `\"roles\": [\"ops\"]`, only callers with the `ops` role get the detailed report, so a public endpoint doesn't reveal response times and entity names.\n\n**Source:** [health checks](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/monitor/health-checks) · [runtime](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/runtime)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["Each data source is checked with a simple query, compared against `threshold-ms` (default 1,000).","Each REST- or GraphQL-enabled entity is checked by reading its first N rows (default 100).","Stored procedure entities are included in the entity checks.","In production mode, `runtime.health.roles` must list who can see the detailed report."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["Yes","Yes","No","Yes"]}},{"id":"dab-10.4-1","domain":2,"section":"10","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 10.4 · Telemetry and logging","scenario":"","stem":"Match each telemetry target to its `runtime.telemetry` block.","type":"dragdrop","format":"DRAG DROP","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"`application-insights` needs a `connection-string`. `open-telemetry` needs an `endpoint` (exporter `grpc` by default, or `httpprotobuf`; default service name `dab`). `azure-log-analytics` needs the DCR immutable ID, the DCE endpoint and a custom table. `file` writes rolling logs. `log-level` sets levels per namespace.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`application-insights`:** `{ \"enabled\": true, \"connection-string\": \"@env('APPINSIGHTS_CONN')\" }`. Requests and dependencies then show up in Application Insights.\n- **`open-telemetry`:** `{ \"endpoint\": \"http://otel-collector:4317\", \"service-name\": \"dab-library\" }`, for Jaeger, Grafana or any OTLP backend.\n- **`azure-log-analytics`:** sends logs to a custom table (such as `DabLogs_CL`) through a data collection endpoint and rule.\n- **`file`:** rolling log files on disk, for an air-gapped server with no cloud monitoring.\n- **`event-hubs`** isn't a telemetry target. To get DAB logs into Event Hubs, send them to Log Analytics or an OpenTelemetry collector that exports there.\n\n**Source:** [application insights](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/monitor/application-insights) · [open telemetry](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/monitor/open-telemetry) · [runtime](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/runtime)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","dd":{"targets":["Azure Monitor Application Insights","An OpenTelemetry collector","A Log Analytics custom table via a data collection rule","A rolling local log file"],"values":["application-insights","azure-log-analytics","event-hubs","file","open-telemetry"],"answer":["application-insights","open-telemetry","azure-log-analytics","file"]}},{"id":"dab-10.4-2","domain":2,"section":"10","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 10.4 · Telemetry and logging","scenario":"","stem":"Which setting makes DAB send traces and metrics to an OpenTelemetry collector over HTTP instead of gRPC?","type":"single","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The OpenTelemetry exporter defaults to `grpc`; `httpprotobuf` switches to HTTP. Set `endpoint` (required), optional `headers` and `service-name` (default `dab`). `dab add-telemetry` configures these from the CLI.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **`exporter-protocol: \"httpprotobuf\"`:** for example, a collector that only listens on `http://collector:4318` (the OTLP/HTTP port), or a proxy that blocks gRPC.\n- **`file.enabled: true`** writes logs to local files. It doesn't change how OpenTelemetry exports.\n- **`log-level: \"Debug\"`** changes *how much* is logged, not where or how it's sent.\n- **`host.mode: \"development\"`** turns on developer features (Swagger, Nitro, detailed errors). It has nothing to do with telemetry transport.\n\n**Source:** [application insights](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/monitor/application-insights) · [open telemetry](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/monitor/open-telemetry) · [runtime](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/runtime)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"`runtime.telemetry.open-telemetry.exporter-protocol: \"httpprotobuf\"`"},{"key":"B","text":"`runtime.telemetry.file.enabled: true`"},{"key":"C","text":"`runtime.telemetry.log-level: \"Debug\"`"},{"key":"D","text":"`runtime.host.mode: \"development\"`"}],"answer":["A"],"answerMd":""},{"id":"dab-10.5-1","domain":2,"section":"10","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 10.5 · Timeouts and connection resiliency","scenario":"","stem":"Answer Yes or No for each statement about timeouts and resiliency.","type":"hotspot","format":"HOTSPOT","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":true,"explanation":"There's **no** DAB-wide timeout: use database connection-string parameters (for SQL Server, the command timeout), which apply to REST, GraphQL and MCP. DAB retries transient errors up to five times with exponential backoff. The MCP aggregate tool has `query-timeout` (1–600 seconds).\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n- **A global `runtime.query-timeout`** is false. There's no such setting in DAB.\n- **Command timeout in the connection string:** `Server=...;Database=Library;Command Timeout=60;` gives every REST, GraphQL and MCP query 60 seconds (SqlClient's default is 30).\n- **Retries with backoff:** if Azure SQL returns a transient error such as 40613 during a failover, DAB waits 2 s and tries again, then 4, 8, 16 and 32 s, before giving up.\n- **`aggregate-records` timeout:** `\"runtime\": { \"mcp\": { \"dml-tools\": { \"aggregate-records\": { \"query-timeout\": 120 } } } }` lets an agent's big `GROUP BY` run longer than the default 30 seconds.\n\n**Source:** [timeout](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/concept/database/timeout) · [data source](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/configuration/data-source)","options":[],"answer":[],"answerMd":"","hs":{"statements":["DAB has a global `runtime.query-timeout` setting.","You set the SQL command timeout in the connection string.","DAB retries transient database errors with exponential backoff (2, 4, 8, 16, 32 seconds).","The MCP `aggregate-records` tool has its own query timeout (default 30 seconds)."],"options":[["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"],["Yes","No"]],"answer":["No","Yes","Yes","Yes"]}},{"id":"dab-11.1-1","domain":2,"section":"11","source":"dab","sourceLabel":"DAB 11.1 · Containers and Azure hosting options","scenario":"","stem":"You deploy DAB to Azure Container Apps with a custom image. Which statements are true? Each correct answer is part of the solution.","type":"multi","format":"","confidence":"docs","noShuffle":false,"explanation":"The official image is `mcr.microsoft.com/azure-databases/data-api-builder`, and DAB reads `/App/dab-config.json`. Build with Azure Container Registry, then run it on Container Apps. DAB also runs on App Service, Container Instances, AKS, locally in Docker, from the CLI, air-gapped, or from source.\n\n---\n\n**🧪 The other options, by example**\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/azure-databases/data-api-builder:latest\nCOPY dab-config.json /App/dab-config.json\n```\n\n```bash\naz acr build -r contosoacr -t dab-library:1.0 .\naz containerapp create -g rg -n dab-library --image contosoacr.azurecr.io/dab-library:1.0 \\\n  --secrets sql-conn=\"Server=...\" --env-vars SQL_CONN=secretref:sql-conn --ingress external --target-port 5000\n```\n\n- **Official base image** and **`/App/dab-config.json`:** the engine looks for the config there, so the image needs no command-line arguments.\n- **Secrets outside the image:** the config says `@env('SQL_CONN')`, and Container Apps fills it from a secret. The image itself holds no password, so it's safe in the registry.\n- **Only Azure Container Apps** is false. The same image runs on App Service, Container Instances, AKS or any Docker host, and the CLI can run DAB directly on a VM or a laptop.\n\n**Source:** [deployment](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/deployment/) · [azure container apps](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/deployment/azure-container-apps) · [local container](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-api-builder/deployment/local-container)","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Base the image on `mcr.microsoft.com/azure-databases/data-api-builder`"},{"key":"B","text":"Copy the configuration to `/App/dab-config.json` in the image"},{"key":"C","text":"Keep secrets out of the image with `@env()` and Container Apps secrets or environment variables"},{"key":"D","text":"DAB can only be hosted in Azure Container Apps"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"answerMd":""}]};
